<p>The 70th Eurovision Song Contest opens Tuesday in Vienna with its first semifinal, with Israel still in the competition and five public broadcasters from Spain, Ireland, Slovenia, the Netherlands and Iceland absent after withdrawing in protest over Israel’s participation during the Gaza war. Public broadcasters in Spain, Ireland and Slovenia also said they would not air the contest, and the grand final is scheduled for Saturday. The European Broadcasting Union allowed Israel to compete, saying Israel met eligibility requirements, despite pressure from several broadcasters to bar it over the war in Gaza and concerns about voting campaigns. Israel is represented by Noam Bettan, who is due to perform the ballad “Michelle” in Tuesday’s first semifinal. The contest opens under revised voting rules and scrutiny of Israeli promotional efforts. The EBU issued a formal warning over videos urging viewers outside Israel to use their full voting allowance for the Israeli entry, saying they were not in line with competition rules or the spirit of the event. A New York Times investigation said Israel had used public money, diplomacy and multilingual advertising around Eurovision and spent at least $1 million on marketing, while reporting no evidence that Israel used bots or covert methods to affect the overall result.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/P4L2JvH6GeR70KBdT9kVyfD4Eafw46k4mx81LiJUt_bh-yMAZu6DAmdJw_qmbqQEGF4sbNEVCI0oIzl6p9RRuG-MfywQVaLNdFnMWpn1ifDHYUx93X63y-rHq10NRSOkHsZeggMBk2HXQ1rmsjU0zp85_naQYpX2vack8ed1xG03qexfyIThEjMgNtla4K_EsZ_Lwfye8xdf06MktW90Cyw2Gle4MB8PQYkkpGe_sYR6A-ObRtf1prxaJ2LyzL99qjKA05VlJK6F8b4U_Q' alt='Noam Bettan, representing Israel, attends the 2026 Eurovision Song Contest&#x27;s “Turquoise Carpet” event in Vienna, Austria, May 10, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Broadcast replacements: Ireland’s RTÉ planned alternative programming, including a Eurovision-themed “Father Ted” episode, and Spain’s RTVE scheduled “La Casa de la Música”.</li><li>Press-access concern: Infobae reported that some broadcasters also raised concerns about press freedom because Israel prevents their journalists from entering Gaza.</li><li>Participation level: Reports put this year’s field at 35 competing countries, with Infobae describing it as the lowest number of participants since 2004.</li><li>Prior result: Israel’s Yuval Raphael finished second in 2025 after topping the public vote, a result that helped drive complaints about audience-vote mobilization before this year’s rule changes.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>European Broadcasting Union: The EBU said it contacted Kan within 20 minutes of seeing videos instructing viewers to use their full voting allowance for Israel and asked the delegation to stop distributing and remove them. The EBU said the videos did not appear to be part of a large-scale funded third-party campaign. (<a href='https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/eurovision-2026-israel-formal-warning_uk_6a01a1f3e4b06e786e3f41d3'>HuffPost UK</a>)</li><li>Kan, Israel’s public broadcaster: Kan representatives said no rules were broken and described the videos as material from the artists themselves, in line with promotional activity by other contestants. (<a href='https://www.ruv.is/frettir/menning-og-daegurmal/2026-05-11-ebu-aminnir-eurovision-flytjanda-israels-474739/'>RÚV</a>)</li><li>RTV Slovenia: RTV Slovenia said its national programming would replace what it called the “Eurovision circus” with the themed series “Voices of Palestine.” (<a href='https://www.infobae.com/america/mundo/2026/05/11/espana-irlanda-y-eslovenia-boicotean-eurovision-y-no-retransmitiran-el-festival-por-la-participacion-de-israel/'>Infobae</a>)</li><li>Israel’s delegation: Der Standard reported that Israel’s team was trying to present Bettan’s entry as an apolitical heartbreak song and avoid being drawn into the political debate around the contest. (<a href='https://www.derstandard.at/story/3000000320293/israel-will-die-s